---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SatelliteGlobe from '../components/SatelliteGlobe.tsx';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Live Satellite Viewer — Orbital Notes"
  description="A real-time 3D view of satellites orbiting Earth, propagated in your browser from live CelesTrak data."
  wide
>
  <section class="mb-8">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink-900 sm:text-4xl" style="font-family: var(--font-display)">
      Live satellite viewer
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg text-ink-500">
      Everything you read about in the series, made literal. This globe pulls
      live orbital elements from <a
        class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700"
        href="https://celestrak.org/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">CelesTrak</a
      > and propagates each satellite in your browser with the
      <strong class="text-ink-900">SGP4</strong> model — the same math used by mission
      controllers — to place thousands of objects in real time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <SatelliteGlobe client:only="react" />

  <section class="prose mt-4">
    <h2>How this works</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Data:</strong> two-line element sets (TLEs) fetched live from CelesTrak.
        If the request is blocked or offline, a small bundled snapshot is used so
        something always renders.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Propagation:</strong>
        <code>satellite.js</code> runs SGP4 entirely client-side to turn each TLE into
        an Earth-centred inertial position for the current (simulated) time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rendering:</strong> Three.js draws the Earth (NASA Blue Marble) inside
        a NASA deep-star-map sky, with the globe spinning at sidereal rate beneath
        the orbits.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>View modes:</strong> <em>True scale</em> places every satellite at
        its real altitude, so the dense low-Earth-orbit shells crowd against the
        globe while distant GEO objects sit far out. <em>Shell view</em> applies
        a compressed, log-style radial scale — a visualization aid, not a physical
        one — that stretches those low altitudes so the orbital shells fan out into
        visible bands. Both come from the same data; only the altitude-to-distance
        mapping changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Controls:</strong> the ☰ menu over the canvas switches view mode,
        toggles layers (satellites, orbit trace, starfield, Earth) on or off, and
        resets the camera.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Colour coding:</strong> every dot is tinted by category — Starlink,
        OneWeb, space stations, navigation (GPS/GLONASS/Galileo/BeiDou), Iridium,
        and a catch-all "other" — so the shells you see in <em>Shell view</em> are
        easy to tell apart. The colour key under the controls lists only the
        categories present in the currently loaded group, with live counts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hosting:</strong> it is all static and runs on GitHub Pages — no server,
        no build-time data.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Geographic registration is approximate; this is a visualization, not a
      tracking tool. For precise pass predictions you would also model
      observer location, light-time, and atmospheric refraction.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
